ons inside the map that have a well-determined rate. After that, radar and GPS vertical rates differ by 3.3 mm a year (root mean square). That disagreement is included in every rate's uncertainty. Counting all 38 stations, including those outside the map or with a very uncertain GPS rate (marked *), the difference is 3.7 mm a year. Some GPS records ended before the radar record began in 2016, so part of the difference is the two instruments measuring different years."},"nisar":{"ok":true,"summary":"NISAR's L-band radar passed over Galveston 9 times between June 2026 and October 2026. Adding its 8 consecutive interferograms gives the ground motion over those 108 days. Over that time the long-term rates predict that neighborhoods should differ from each other by only about 2.4 mm. NISAR's neighborhood values scatter by about 8 mm. That scatter is the atmosphere and ionosphere delaying the radar signal, not the ground moving, so NISAR cannot yet confirm or contradict the long-term rates. It needs a longer record before the noise averages down to millimeters. What NISAR shows already is reach. In a typical 12-day pair it returned a measurement over 86% of the land in these neighborhoods (between 85% and 86%), where the Sentinel-1 record gives a reliable long-term rate over 61%. Each figure uses its own product's quality flags, so this is not a like-for-like test, but it points to NISAR filling in marsh edges and wooded ground that are blank today.","product":"NISAR L2 GUNW (provisional)","doi":"10.5067/NIL2GUNW-P1","track_frame":"012_D_074","band":"L","polarization":"HH","first_date":"20260627","last_date":"20261013","days":108,"n_pairs":8,"neighborhood_scatter_mm":8.5,"expected_range_mm":2.44,"correlation":0.06,"land_measured_typical_pair":0.855,"land_measured_every_pair":0.807,"opera_land_measured":0.614,"n_neighborhoods_compared":79,"pairs":[{"reference":"20260627","secondary":"20260709","granule":"NISAR_L2_PR_GUNW_024_012_D_074_025_4000_SH_20260627T010224_20260627T0